<p>In the latest development, several analyses say the war involving the United States, Israel, and Iran has left Gulf states facing heavy regional fallout. Missile and drone attacks have disrupted the Strait of Hormuz and struck civilian airports, ports, hotels, and refineries across Gulf countries despite the longstanding US security presence there. The result, these reports say, is a sharper sense in Gulf capitals that the US security umbrella has weakened, even as Washington remains deeply involved in the conflict and its economic consequences. The reports also say Iran wants terms that would prevent renewed US or Israeli attacks before it ends the war, signaling that Tehran is preparing for a prolonged confrontation rather than a quick settlement. At the same time, the conflict has reshaped regional politics: the sources say Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu appears politically strengthened, while President Trump is left managing strains with Gulf partners and volatility in global markets.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bargaining strategy: The Financial Times describes Tehran&#x27;s demand for assurances against renewed US or Israeli strikes as an effort to tie any ceasefire to future deterrence, signaling that it does not expect a quick settlement.</li><li>Security model under strain: The South China Morning Post says the bigger implication for Gulf capitals is that prosperity and stability may no longer automatically track the US security architecture they relied on for decades.</li><li>Political realignment: Reuters analysis carried by The Straits Times and Al-Monitor says the war has shifted Israeli debate away from Gaza and toward Iran, where broader consensus gives Netanyahu friendlier political terrain.</li><li>Uneven costs: Reuters analysis says Gulf allies have absorbed some of the heaviest fallout, while President Trump is left managing both allied frustration and the shock to global markets.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iran: Tehran is seeking to end the war only on terms that would reduce the risk of future US or Israeli attacks, indicating that it is prepared to hold out in a protracted conflict. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/869f650b-7a9e-461d-a2bb-0788eb991e6d'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Gulf states: Gulf governments are confronting the possibility that decades of reliance on a US-led security umbrella may no longer protect their territory and civilian infrastructure from spillover from regional war. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3347189/severely-damaged-how-us-israel-war-iran-decoupling-gulf-security'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Israeli political analysts: Analysts cited by Reuters say the conflict has strengthened Benjamin Netanyahu at home by shifting attention to Iran, where there is broader Israeli consensus than on Gaza. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/analysis-iran-war-boosts-netanyahu-bruises-trump-and-gulf-states'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>US administration observers: Reuters analysis says President Trump now faces the task of containing market disruption and repairing strains with Gulf partners after the conflict widened regional insecurity. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-war-boosts-netanyahu-bruises-trump-and-gulf-states'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3347189/severely-damaged-how-us-israel-war-iran-decoupling-gulf-security'>‘Severely damaged’: how the US-Israel war on Iran is decoupling Gulf security</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/869f650b-7a9e-461d-a2bb-0788eb991e6d'>Iran sets its price to end the war</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-war-boosts-netanyahu-bruises-trump-and-gulf-states'>Iran war boosts Netanyahu, but bruises Trump and Gulf states</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/analysis-iran-war-boosts-netanyahu-bruises-trump-and-gulf-states'>Analysis-Iran war boosts Netanyahu, bruises Trump and Gulf states</a> - al-monitor.com</li></ul>